The comparator contract, verified:

```js
// NEGATIVE → a comes first; POSITIVE → b first; ZERO → keep order (stable)
[10, 9, 1].sort();                  // [1, 10, 9] — STRINGS by default!
[10, 9, 1].sort((a, b) => a - b);   // [1, 9, 10] — numeric

// DESCENDING — flip the subtraction
scores.sort((a, b) => b - a);        // [10, 9, 1]

// SORT BY DERIVED KEY
words.sort((a, b) => a.length - b.length);      // by length
users.sort((a, b) => a.createdAt - b.createdAt); // by date (numeric ms)

// MULTI-KEY — tie-break chains
items.sort((a, b) =>
  b.priority - a.priority                    // primary: desc
  || a.name.localeCompare(b.name)            // tie: name asc
);
```

Why the default lies: with no comparator, sort converts to strings and compares UTF-16 code units — numbers order lexicographically (10 < 9 because '1' < '9'), and locale differences (é, accents, case) sort wrong; localeCompare handles human-language ordering ('é' near 'e', case-insensitive per locale rules).

The interview patterns (when sorting is the ANSWER): (1) sort-then-scan for duplicates/gaps — after sorting, equal elements are ADJACENT and a gap is nums[i+1] - nums[i] > 1 — one pass O(n log n) instead of nested O(n²) includes; (2) sort-then-two-pointers — pair-sum and merge-interval families reduce to sorted order first; (3) the greedy exchanges — scheduling by deadline, meeting rooms by start time: sort is the setup step that makes a greedy single pass provably correct.

Mutability warning (the JS-specific gotcha): sort MUTATES the array — copy first ([...arr].sort() or toSorted) when the original order or React state is still needed.
